#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133487 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1fba8401-2da1-50f2-8e96-84cbe06d95fc" :knowledge-point/title "Applying Properties of the Dot Product"}] :question/id #uuid "c374456a-bff7-5145-a590-620bbf50dabf" :question/math-academy-id "e-5536" :question/problem "Find $(5\\mathbf{a}+3\\mathbf{b}) \\cdot \\mathbf{c}+(4\\mathbf{b}+2\\mathbf{c}) \\cdot \\mathbf{d},$ if $\\mathbf{c} \\cdot \\mathbf{a} = 2,$ $\\mathbf{b} \\cdot \\mathbf{c} = -1,$ $\\mathbf{b} \\cdot \\mathbf{d} = 2,$ and $\\mathbf{d} \\cdot \\mathbf{c}=3.$" :question/worked-solution "Using the fact that the dot product is linear in the first variable, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(5a+3b)\\cdot c+(4b+2c)\\cdot d & =(5a)\\cdot c+(3b)\\cdot c+(4b)\\cdot d+(2c)\\cdot d \\\\  & =5(a\\cdot c)+3(b\\cdot c)+4(b\\cdot d)+2(c\\cdot d).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we can substitute the values for $\\mathbf{b} \\cdot \\mathbf{c}$ and $\\mathbf{b} \\cdot \\mathbf{d},$ and use the fact that the dot product is symmetric to get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5(a\\cdot c)+3(b\\cdot c)+4(b\\cdot d)+2(c\\cdot d) & =5(c\\cdot a)+3(-1)+4(2)+2(d\\cdot c) \\\\  & =5(c\\cdot a)-3+8+2(d\\cdot c) \\\\  & =5(c\\cdot a)+5+2(d\\cdot c).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting the values for $\\mathbf{c} \\cdot \\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{d} \\cdot \\mathbf{c},$ we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5(c\\cdot a)+5+2(d\\cdot c) & =5(2)+5+2(3) \\\\  & =10+5+6 \\\\  & =21.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $(5\\mathbf{a}+3\\mathbf{b}) \\cdot \\mathbf{c}+(4\\mathbf{b}+2\\mathbf{c}) \\cdot \\mathbf{d} = 21.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133488 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "41c2d289-8df5-551b-9501-3a762c67386f" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About the Dot Product"}] :question/id #uuid "13909d3e-b3df-5fcb-9056-5d71283fbebe" :question/math-academy-id "e-5537" :question/problem "Suppose $(4\\mathbf{a} + 2\\mathbf{b}) \\cdot \\mathbf{a} = {0},$ where $\\mathbf{a} \\neq \\mathbf{0}.$ Which of the following statements are true? \n\n- $\\mathbf{b} \\cdot \\mathbf{a} = -2(\\mathbf{a} \\cdot \\mathbf{a})$\n\n- $\\mathbf{a} \\cdot \\mathbf{a} =  0$\n\n- $\\mathbf{b} \\cdot \\mathbf{a} = 1$" :question/worked-solution "Let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Simplifying, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(4a+2b)\\cdot a & =0 \\\\ (4a)\\cdot a+(2b)\\cdot a & =0 \\\\ 4(a\\cdot a)+2(b\\cdot a) & =0 \\\\ 2(b\\cdot a) & =-4(a\\cdot a) \\\\ b\\cdot a & =-2(a\\cdot a).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Since $\\mathbf{a} \\neq \\mathbf{0},$ we must have $\\mathbf{a} \\cdot \\mathbf{a} > 0.$\n\n- Statement III is false since it leads to a contradiction. Simplifying $(4\\mathbf{a} + 2\\mathbf{b}) \\cdot \\mathbf{a} = {0}$ and substituting in $\\mathbf{b} \\cdot \\mathbf{a} = 1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(4a+2b)\\cdot a & =0 \\\\ 4a\\cdot a+2b\\cdot a & =0 \\\\ 4a\\cdot a+2(1) & =0 \\\\ a\\cdot a & =-\\frac{1}{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nbut this is a contradiction since $\\mathbf{a} \\neq \\mathbf{0}$ implies that $\\mathbf{a} \\cdot \\mathbf{a} > 0.$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the correct answer is \"I only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133551 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "98eb5973-f9b1-5c2f-8763-e8f8707e9b89" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Dot Product"}] :question/id #uuid "7000f188-3223-5a2c-afe6-45fd3f92b8c6" :question/math-academy-id "e-5907" :question/problem "If ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044469/assets/8d9347b89864ea011c9dd3fbd17b94f05c479ff7a96343aae7928b241491c3ba.png) ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044469/assets/b5521921f5997e3cb748c518a9823574fcdfb82cd4bb1d1f7bbbf17d2f9ac06a.png) and $\\mathbf{v} \\cdot \\mathbf{v} = 34,$ then what is $\\dfrac{\\mathbf{u}\\cdot 2\\mathbf{v}}{3\\mathbf{v}\\cdot \\mathbf{v}}?$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the fact that the dot product is linear.\n\nTo quickly compute the numerator $\\mathbf{u} \\cdot 2\\mathbf{v},$ we compute $\\mathbf{u} \\cdot \\mathbf{v}$ first and then multiply the result by $2.$ Doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot 2v & =2(u\\cdot v) \\\\  & =2({u}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}+{u}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{2}+{u}_{3}\\cdot {v}_{3}+{u}_{4}\\cdot {v}_{4}) \\\\  & =2(7\\cdot 1+5\\cdot 2+(-9)\\cdot (-5)+3\\cdot (-2)) \\\\  & =2(7+10+45-6) \\\\  & =2\\cdot 56 \\\\  & =112.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo quickly compute the denominator $3\\mathbf{v} \\cdot \\mathbf{v},$ we multiply $\\mathbf{v} \\cdot \\mathbf{v}$ by $3.$ Doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3v\\cdot v & =3(v\\cdot v) \\\\  & =3\\cdot 34 \\\\  & =102.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathbf{u}\\cdot 2\\mathbf{v}}{3\\mathbf{v}\\cdot\\mathbf{v}} =\\dfrac{112}{102}=\\dfrac{56}{51}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323045 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "98eb5973-f9b1-5c2f-8763-e8f8707e9b89" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Dot Product"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f0c606fc-97c9-5343-87fc-60d3dab583e9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{34}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323047} {:answer/id #uuid "1a7b89fa-b259-589b-9fe4-16a0463e92d6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{7}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323048} {:answer/id #uuid "ec3e3dfe-3432-5581-a312-a50cbae61eda" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-34" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323049} {:answer/id #uuid "00b360d1-ea2e-5ef9-8311-da8f3ccf2152" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{7}{25}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323050} {:answer/id #uuid "7e754999-1912-52f7-a5f3-9949f2c1c868" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{17}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323051}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7e754999-1912-52f7-a5f3-9949f2c1c868" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{17}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323051} :answer-field/id #uuid "b5473c63-f1e1-520f-a85c-620d7704c9fc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323046}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c6000e94-92ff-51a9-88f5-c3892fb8a07e" :question/math-academy-id "q-139948" :question/problem "If $u=[\\begin{aligned}9 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 7 \\\\ 8 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\,,$ $v=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]\\,,$ and $u\\cdot v=-1,$ then $\\frac{(5u)\\cdot v}{v\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2}v)}=$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the fact that the dot product is linear.\n\nTo quickly compute the numerator $(5u)\\cdot v,$ we multiply $u\\cdot v$ by $5.$ Doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(5u)\\cdot v & =5(u\\cdot v) \\\\  & =5\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo quickly compute the denominator $v\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2}v),$ we compute $v\\cdot v$ first and then multiply the result by $\\frac{1}{2}.$ Doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2}v) & =\\frac{1}{2}(v\\cdot v) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}({v}_{1}^{2}+{v}_{2}^{2}+{v}_{3}^{2}+{v}_{4}^{2}+{v}_{5}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}({1}^{2}+(-2)^{2}+(-2)^{2}+{0}^{2}+{5}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(1+4+4+0+25) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 34 \\\\  & =17.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{(5u)\\cdot v}{v\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2}v)}=-\\frac{5}{17}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323052 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1fba8401-2da1-50f2-8e96-84cbe06d95fc" :knowledge-point/title "Applying Properties of the Dot Product"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f9dbe92e-d70b-5239-a9a6-8f4cf1b2b356" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "40" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323054} {:answer/id #uuid "e358773f-b4a4-52d1-99d4-7cb61e39d702" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-56" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323055} {:answer/id #uuid "0f940e14-8e0a-5a6a-83f2-bcb09c778b6e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "72" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323056} {:answer/id #uuid "b9348e5a-fa20-5d2c-aa0b-c02382e777f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-60" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323057} {:answer/id #uuid "7b991c0b-43e5-5e40-a38c-b7af7ae0c330" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-48" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323058}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7b991c0b-43e5-5e40-a38c-b7af7ae0c330" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-48" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323058} :answer-field/id #uuid "ade66564-dcc9-5bc7-b541-cf2ce46c61b3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323053}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "54ff819f-c769-5521-bdf5-51f70981bea2" :question/math-academy-id "q-139951" :question/problem "If $\\alpha \\beta =-8$ and $b\\cdot a=\\frac{15}{2}$, then what is $(\\alpha a)\\cdot (\\beta b)+12?$" :question/worked-solution "Using the fact that the dot product is linear in both variables, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\alpha a)\\cdot (\\beta b)+12 & =\\alpha (a\\cdot \\beta b)+12 \\\\  & =\\alpha \\beta (a\\cdot b)+12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, using that the dot product is symmetric, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\alpha \\beta (a\\cdot b)+12 & =\\alpha \\beta (b\\cdot a)+12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute the values for $\\alpha \\beta $ and $b\\cdot a:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\alpha \\beta (b\\cdot a)+12 & =(-8)\\cdot \\frac{15}{2}+12 \\\\  & =-60+12 \\\\  & =-48\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323059 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "41c2d289-8df5-551b-9501-3a762c67386f" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About the Dot Product"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "98af5ec6-aa41-5b14-97b5-d2bd6125cd35" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323061} {:answer/id #uuid "a760c7ea-d979-5c1e-a9ff-edaf88c632af" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323062} {:answer/id #uuid "a4073eff-a13a-5181-936d-7d8711b3fd00" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323063} {:answer/id #uuid "184ffb72-e7cb-538c-8cbd-582aa4d40188" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323064} {:answer/id #uuid "c0398650-8ad4-59f4-8870-33385031b8f2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323065}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "98af5ec6-aa41-5b14-97b5-d2bd6125cd35" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323061} :answer-field/id #uuid "3b41db8b-1baa-5df8-9618-5b209121fe17" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323060}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6c7267a9-814c-5af4-b1fd-9515f0fed3e3" :question/math-academy-id "q-139957" :question/problem "Suppose $a\\cdot (a+b)=0$, where $a\\ne 0.$ Which of the following statements are true? \n\n- $a\\cdot a<0$\n- $b\\cdot a>0$\n- $b\\cdot a=5$" :question/worked-solution "Let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Indeed, $a\\ne 0$ implies that $a\\cdot a>0.$\n\n- Statement II and III are false. Simplifying the expression $a\\cdot (a+b)=0$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot (a+b) & =0 \\\\ a\\cdot a+a\\cdot b & =0 \\\\ a\\cdot a+b\\cdot a & =0 \\\\ b\\cdot a & =-a\\cdot a\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nSince $a\\ne 0$ implies that $a\\cdot a>0$, we obtain that $b\\cdot a<0.$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the correct answer is \"None of the statements are true\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323066 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1fba8401-2da1-50f2-8e96-84cbe06d95fc" :knowledge-point/title "Applying Properties of the Dot Product"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a84b8796-cf7c-5a2e-a6e6-4ac46c5c11d6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323068} {:answer/id #uuid "0fbbdfb5-230c-5c60-b459-60d2ec3363e8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323069} {:answer/id #uuid "41202990-aff1-56c0-a0af-9777c22a5773" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "26" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323070} {:answer/id #uuid "c7c8ce62-97dd-5003-8fbc-ffbb8e479943" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323071} {:answer/id #uuid "7f311bdb-932e-525d-b167-524fe1dd8aee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "23" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323072}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "41202990-aff1-56c0-a0af-9777c22a5773" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "26" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323070} :answer-field/id #uuid "77e50938-562f-5646-9e91-fdd629f75ee3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323067}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "34af850c-ea7c-55c3-b564-f39c0446abaa" :question/math-academy-id "q-39196" :question/problem "Find $(2a+5b)\\cdot c+(b+3c)\\cdot d,$ if $c\\cdot a=2,$ $b\\cdot c=3,$ $b\\cdot d=4$ and $d\\cdot c=1.$" :question/worked-solution "Using the fact that the dot product is linear in the first variable, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2a+5b)\\cdot c+(b+3c)\\cdot d & =(2a)\\cdot c+(5b)\\cdot c+b\\cdot d+(3c)\\cdot d \\\\  & =2(a\\cdot c)+5(b\\cdot c)+b\\cdot d+3(c\\cdot d).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we can use the fact that the dot product is symmetric to get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(a\\cdot c)+5(b\\cdot c)+b\\cdot d+3(c\\cdot d) & =2(c\\cdot a)+5(b\\cdot c)+b\\cdot d+3(d\\cdot c).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting the values for $c\\cdot a,$ $b\\cdot c,$ $b\\cdot d,$ and $d\\cdot c,$ we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(c\\cdot a)+5(b\\cdot c)+b\\cdot d+3(d\\cdot c) & =2(2)+5(3)+4+3(1) \\\\  & =4+15+4+3 \\\\  & =26.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $(2a+5b)\\cdot c+(b+3c)\\cdot d=26.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134122 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1fba8401-2da1-50f2-8e96-84cbe06d95fc" :knowledge-point/title "Applying Properties of the Dot Product"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ac80673b-411c-5163-b208-8bf7d3f3cc03" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131175} {:answer/id #uuid "11e8f4f3-3eee-539f-be28-2951832419da" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131176} {:answer/id #uuid "2ad8ac6d-1ec4-5ae1-a4f3-3ca0cf31eafc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131177} {:answer/id #uuid "56dffa35-c327-550b-b4ed-4bcd0acb1cb6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131178} {:answer/id #uuid "644ea17e-d548-59b3-a221-3676f4c3376e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131179}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2ad8ac6d-1ec4-5ae1-a4f3-3ca0cf31eafc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131177} :answer-field/id #uuid "d159b7ba-de36-5978-83a2-e9dff9d080e6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131174}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "85c8d44f-ab83-5536-bc23-d67925596d64" :question/math-academy-id "q-39202" :question/problem "Find $(3a+b)\\cdot c+(2b-2c)\\cdot d,$ if $c\\cdot a=3,$ $c\\cdot b=-2,$ $b\\cdot d=-1,$ and $d\\cdot c=2.$" :question/worked-solution "Using the fact that the dot product is linear in the first variable, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(3a+b)\\cdot c+(2b-2c)\\cdot d & =(3a)\\cdot c+b\\cdot c+(2b)\\cdot d-(2c)\\cdot d \\\\  & =3(a\\cdot c)+b\\cdot c+2(b\\cdot d)-2(c\\cdot d).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we can use the fact that the dot product is symmetric to get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3(a\\cdot c)+b\\cdot c+2(b\\cdot d)-2(c\\cdot d) & =3(c\\cdot a)+c\\cdot b+2(b\\cdot d)-2(d\\cdot c).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting the values for $c\\cdot a,$ $c\\cdot b,$ $b\\cdot d,$ and $d\\cdot c,$ we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3(c\\cdot a)+c\\cdot b+2(b\\cdot d)-2(d\\cdot c) & =3(3)+(-2)+2(-1)-2(2) \\\\  & =9-2-2-4 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $(3a+b)\\cdot c+(2b-2c)\\cdot d=1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323073 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "41c2d289-8df5-551b-9501-3a762c67386f" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About the Dot Product"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b2140f62-47ac-539a-9423-09a170ae82c2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323075} {:answer/id #uuid "7d01f756-8551-5842-bbf1-3f80189be14d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323076} {:answer/id #uuid "a5276852-d117-5398-8e8f-c22f1ce06f18" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323077} {:answer/id #uuid "ac8fdb0e-e4db-56e5-bef4-1bea565659f0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323078} {:answer/id #uuid "f912d2ec-a9f6-53a5-9dd7-af62ba1b92aa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323079}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b2140f62-47ac-539a-9423-09a170ae82c2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323075} :answer-field/id #uuid "233ad840-2e99-5b7e-86a7-cd882deea2d0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323074}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a36b9f56-d20b-5fa5-91b5-434374e4c66c" :question/math-academy-id "q-39203" :question/problem "Suppose $(a+b)\\cdot a=0$, where $a\\ne 0.$ Which of the following statements are true? \n\n- $b\\cdot a>0$\n- $b\\cdot a=0$\n- $a\\cdot a=0$" :question/worked-solution "Let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Statements I and II are false. Simplifying the expression $(a+b)\\cdot a=0$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(a+b)\\cdot a & =0 \\\\ a\\cdot a+b\\cdot a & =0 \\\\ b\\cdot a & =-a\\cdot a\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nSince $a\\ne 0$ implies that $a\\cdot a>0$, we obtain that $-a\\cdot a<0.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $a\\ne 0,$ we must have $a\\cdot a>0.$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that none of the statements are true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134124 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "98eb5973-f9b1-5c2f-8763-e8f8707e9b89" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Dot Product"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b1b4eb15-da66-59d8-ba2f-7c923bb902e7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131187} {:answer/id #uuid "c3127ec9-d1ca-531c-8b7e-1f52ad89c001" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131188} {:answer/id #uuid "1a5642a4-92b2-5082-974e-cba121993fb9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131189} {:answer/id #uuid "1ba49700-af69-523d-afe7-a9462360a76b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131190} {:answer/id #uuid "74566f91-cd06-507e-a39c-8a9f7b1c25aa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131191}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c3127ec9-d1ca-531c-8b7e-1f52ad89c001" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131188} :answer-field/id #uuid "96f95a03-5a4d-5fe8-a222-3f69319730f4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131186}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "687f42ad-4d31-5616-a543-616339685f35" :question/math-academy-id "q-39205" :question/problem "If $u=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\,,$ $v=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\,,$ and $u\\cdot u=15,$ then $\\frac{u\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2}u)}{u\\cdot (\\frac{3}{4}v)}=$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the fact that the dot product is linear.\n\nTo quickly compute the numerator $u\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2}u),$ we multiply $u\\cdot u$ by $\\frac{1}{2}.$ Doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2}u) & =\\frac{1}{2}(u\\cdot u) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 15 \\\\  & =\\frac{15}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo quickly compute the denominator $u\\cdot (\\frac{3}{4}v),$ we compute $u\\cdot v$ first and then multiply the result by $\\frac{3}{4}.$ Doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot (\\frac{3}{4}v) & =\\frac{3}{4}(u\\cdot v) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}({u}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}+{u}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{2}+{u}_{3}\\cdot {v}_{3}+{u}_{4}\\cdot {v}_{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}((-1)\\cdot (-3)+(-3)\\cdot (-1)+(-2)\\cdot (-2)+1\\cdot 2) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}(3+3+4+2) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}\\cdot 12 \\\\  & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{u\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2}u)}{u\\cdot (\\frac{3}{4}v)} & =\\frac{(\\frac{15}{2})}{9}=\\frac{5}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323080 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1fba8401-2da1-50f2-8e96-84cbe06d95fc" :knowledge-point/title "Applying Properties of the Dot Product"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "912b2beb-6f91-5034-b4e8-7878265ead67" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323082} {:answer/id #uuid "3a4d1684-07de-5eb7-aba2-59c8343bd8c6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-30" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323083} {:answer/id #uuid "afef7dbf-20d8-52ff-9741-029e377f93f7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323084} {:answer/id #uuid "787a9925-7181-5560-837d-adf6af05ebe6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "48" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323085} {:answer/id #uuid "eb9586c2-2d08-57b9-ae55-ebd1c9aa7f7e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323086}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "912b2beb-6f91-5034-b4e8-7878265ead67" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323082} :answer-field/id #uuid "8a185a11-3f33-5a77-bc88-20bfb09f1ab6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323081}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e56fe336-6e05-5cc3-bc96-23a17fa637d1" :question/math-academy-id "q-40049" :question/problem "If $\\alpha \\beta =-5$ and $a\\cdot b=3$, then what is $(\\alpha b)\\cdot (\\beta a)-9?$" :question/worked-solution "Using the fact that the dot product is linear in both variables, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\alpha b)\\cdot (\\beta a)-9 & =\\alpha (b\\cdot \\beta a)-9 \\\\  & =\\alpha \\beta (b\\cdot a)-9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, using that the dot product is symmetric, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\alpha \\beta (b\\cdot a)-9 & =\\alpha \\beta (a\\cdot b)-9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute the values for $\\alpha \\beta $ and $a\\cdot b:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\alpha \\beta (a\\cdot b)-9 & =(-5)\\cdot (3)-9 \\\\  & =-15-9 \\\\  & =-24\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323087 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "98eb5973-f9b1-5c2f-8763-e8f8707e9b89" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Dot Product"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e9297d59-3ecd-5463-aa95-1b61de806491" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{17}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323089} {:answer/id #uuid "0d24c348-49e2-5257-bccf-3ffdf19f8399" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{16}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323090} {:answer/id #uuid "610b3919-5214-5467-979e-e521ef8fe497" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{17}{18}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323091} {:answer/id #uuid "67c63fc3-637a-54db-8a75-89d1cd0ab0d1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323092} {:answer/id #uuid "2a074d48-c666-5cc9-97fd-19bd3599acbd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323093}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e9297d59-3ecd-5463-aa95-1b61de806491" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{17}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323089} :answer-field/id #uuid "cc880282-2206-55e8-851f-465458cf65cc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323088}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7d7c48e0-b3db-50af-b914-55f8cb8435f4" :question/math-academy-id "q-40108" :question/problem "If $u=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\,,$ $v=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}]\\,,$ and $u\\cdot u=17,$ then $\\frac{u\\cdot (2u)}{u\\cdot (\\frac{1}{3}v)}=$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the fact that the dot product is linear.\n\nTo quickly compute the numerator $u\\cdot (2u),$ we multiply $u\\cdot u$ by $2.$ Doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot (2u) & =2(u\\cdot u) \\\\  & =2\\cdot 17 \\\\  & =34.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo quickly compute the denominator $u\\cdot (\\frac{1}{3}v),$ we compute $u\\cdot v$ first and then multiply the result by $\\frac{1}{3}.$ Doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot (\\frac{1}{3}v) & =\\frac{1}{3}({u}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}+{u}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{2}+{u}_{3}\\cdot {v}_{3}+{u}_{4}\\cdot {v}_{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}((0)\\cdot (1)+3\\cdot (-4)+2\\cdot 2+2\\cdot (-5)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(0-12+4-10) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot (-18) \\\\  & =-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{u\\cdot (2u)}{u\\cdot (\\frac{1}{3}v)}=\\frac{34}{-6}=-\\frac{17}{3}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323094 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "41c2d289-8df5-551b-9501-3a762c67386f" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About the Dot Product"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6d2c0a2d-91a6-5542-8a93-2e3a618e7b7d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323096} {:answer/id #uuid "b725dbac-16d7-58f7-9865-e3b90d2dd6ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323097} {:answer/id #uuid "25454f8f-bd65-57a2-be4e-ad877216cced" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323098} {:answer/id #uuid "93648a40-369c-5ed0-80b9-51c4fce32f8a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "All the listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323099} {:answer/id #uuid "519c4408-78dc-5418-bbc0-db1902d5bdea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323100}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "25454f8f-bd65-57a2-be4e-ad877216cced" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323098} :answer-field/id #uuid "2e80e167-08f9-5c9f-8b35-e2876ab38ab8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323095}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "7fbbc945-8c9d-5dab-b5a7-378aefd270f3" :question/math-academy-id "q-40116" :question/problem "Suppose $(6a-9b)\\cdot a=0$, where $a\\ne 0.$ Which of the following statements are true? \n\n- $a\\cdot a>0$\n- $a\\cdot a=\\frac{1}{2}(b\\cdot a)$\n- $b\\cdot a=-4$" :question/worked-solution "Let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indedd, $a\\ne 0$ implies that $a\\cdot a>0.$\n\n- Statement II is false. Simplifying the expression $(6a-9b)\\cdot a=0$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(6a-9b)\\cdot a & =0 \\\\ (6a)\\cdot a-(9b)\\cdot a & =0 \\\\ 6(a\\cdot a)-9(b\\cdot a) & =0 \\\\ 6(a\\cdot a) & =9(b\\cdot a) \\\\ a\\cdot a & =\\frac{3}{2}(b\\cdot a)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false since it leads to a contradiction. If $b\\cdot a=-4$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(6a-9b)\\cdot a & =0 \\\\ 6(a\\cdot a)-9(b\\cdot a) & =0 \\\\ 6(a\\cdot a)-9(-4) & =0 \\\\ a\\cdot a & =-6,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nbut this is a contradiction since $a\\ne 0$ implies that $a\\cdot a>0.$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the correct answer is \"I only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323101 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "98eb5973-f9b1-5c2f-8763-e8f8707e9b89" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Dot Product"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6d5001dd-7501-5b26-964c-f5835c939b2c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323103} {:answer/id #uuid "3de4debd-54c2-5b5f-9c6d-4eca0da83549" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323104} {:answer/id #uuid "3a532d6e-e8e3-532f-8f31-5d9ac4a34a2c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "30" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323105} {:answer/id #uuid "66937c48-e6cd-5956-acc0-776bacd8cab7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323106} {:answer/id #uuid "385a43c9-b970-551a-8472-e1ecb7db128c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323107}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3de4debd-54c2-5b5f-9c6d-4eca0da83549" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323104} :answer-field/id #uuid "27993627-a4aa-5611-9862-8acc6672f405" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323102}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "526891b7-386c-5932-bad3-5b20c2a854b5" :question/math-academy-id "q-74039" :question/problem "If $u=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]\\,,$ $v=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\\,,$ and $u\\cdot u=30,$ then $\\frac{(\\frac{2}{3}u)\\cdot u}{u\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2}v)}=$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the fact that the dot product is linear.\n\nTo quickly compute the numerator $(\\frac{2}{3}u)\\cdot u,$ we multiply $u\\cdot u$ by $\\frac{2}{3}.$ Doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\frac{2}{3}u)\\cdot u & =\\frac{2}{3}(u\\cdot u) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot 30 \\\\  & =20.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo quickly compute the denominator $u\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2}v),$ we compute $u\\cdot v$ first and then multiply the result by $\\frac{1}{2}.$ Doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2}v) & =\\frac{1}{2}(u\\cdot v) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}({u}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}+{u}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{2}+{u}_{3}\\cdot {v}_{3}+{u}_{4}\\cdot {v}_{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(1\\cdot 1+2\\cdot 1+0\\cdot 2+3\\cdot (-1)+4\\cdot (-1)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(1+2+0-3-4) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (-4) \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{(\\frac{2}{3}u)\\cdot u}{u\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2}v)}=\\frac{20}{-2}=-10.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323108 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "98eb5973-f9b1-5c2f-8763-e8f8707e9b89" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Dot Product"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a735ea3-faa6-5228-bcd5-04ec1d804935" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{18}{25}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323110} {:answer/id #uuid "392050f8-43d5-5087-a20a-aed076684727" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{4}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323111} {:answer/id #uuid "b3eaba96-25bd-5912-b566-c451424a631e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{27}{25}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323112} {:answer/id #uuid "bf7e8d07-1661-5112-8160-d9ce6a62e209" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-75" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323113} {:answer/id #uuid "263f9988-fde7-53ea-9d3a-f9024d34bd61" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "54" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323114}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0a735ea3-faa6-5228-bcd5-04ec1d804935" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{18}{25}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323110} :answer-field/id #uuid "58bf06f6-3da9-53d8-b77d-21710c893510" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323109}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "205a52bf-ab3a-51fb-bc92-9d8428f733fe" :question/math-academy-id "q-74040" :question/problem "If $u=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]\\,,$ $v=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\,,$ and $u\\cdot u=27,$ then $\\frac{(2u)\\cdot u}{u\\cdot (3v)}=$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the fact that the dot product is linear.\n\nTo quickly compute the numerator $(2u)\\cdot u,$ we multiply $u\\cdot u$ by $2.$ Doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2u)\\cdot u & =2(u\\cdot u) \\\\  & =2\\cdot 27 \\\\  & =54.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo quickly compute the denominator $u\\cdot (3v),$ we compute $u\\cdot v$ first and then multiply the result by $3.$ Doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot (3v) & =3(u\\cdot v) \\\\  & =3({u}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}+{u}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{2}+{u}_{3}\\cdot {v}_{3}+{u}_{4}\\cdot {v}_{4}) \\\\  & =3(2\\cdot (-3)+3\\cdot (-2)+1\\cdot (-1)+(-2)\\cdot 3+(-3)\\cdot 2) \\\\  & =3(-6-6-1-6-6) \\\\  & =3\\cdot (-25) \\\\  & =-75.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{(2u)\\cdot u}{u\\cdot (3v)}=\\frac{54}{-75}=-\\frac{18}{25}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323115 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1fba8401-2da1-50f2-8e96-84cbe06d95fc" :knowledge-point/title "Applying Properties of the Dot Product"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d7ee2a66-646a-5dcd-a39a-5e1d2a74818d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323117} {:answer/id #uuid "9eba187a-411b-58bd-8a50-30ce5e8247ab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323118} {:answer/id #uuid "8e596a70-57c8-517c-89e1-33beae96be79" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323119} {:answer/id #uuid "be15516d-6c2f-57d8-b4eb-0b93a11c95e2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-22" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323120} {:answer/id #uuid "997a236a-1437-575f-a06d-6c9403e83723" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323121}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "997a236a-1437-575f-a06d-6c9403e83723" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323121} :answer-field/id #uuid "7298328f-342e-5fe8-a5ba-da2f64db91d2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323116}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f9ad90f2-46d9-558b-96c2-0fbbeabd4cb0" :question/math-academy-id "q-74041" :question/problem "Find $(3a-4b)\\cdot c+(2b)\\cdot d,$ if $c\\cdot a=-2,$ $c\\cdot b=4,$ and $d\\cdot b=5.$" :question/worked-solution "Using the fact that the dot product is linear in the first variable, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(3a-4b)\\cdot c+(2b)\\cdot d & =(3a)\\cdot c-(4b)\\cdot c+(2b)\\cdot d \\\\  & =3(a\\cdot c)-4(b\\cdot c)+2(b\\cdot d).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we can use the fact that the dot product is symmetric to get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3(a\\cdot c)-4(b\\cdot c)+2(b\\cdot d) & =3(c\\cdot a)-4(c\\cdot b)+2(d\\cdot b).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting the values for $c\\cdot a,$ $c\\cdot b$ and $d\\cdot b,$ we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3(c\\cdot a)-4(c\\cdot b)+2(d\\cdot b) & =3(-2)-4(4)+2(5) \\\\  & =-6-16+10 \\\\  & =-12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $(3a-4b)\\cdot c+(2d)\\cdot b=-12.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323122 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "41c2d289-8df5-551b-9501-3a762c67386f" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About the Dot Product"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "92d4841c-aa69-5457-b108-36946ba7fcdc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323124} {:answer/id #uuid "a5e08637-e18a-5c31-b3af-5d9192514dfb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323125} {:answer/id #uuid "1d40c770-b696-5220-9d80-d20edeecdb91" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323126} {:answer/id #uuid "ea13a5d2-1ece-567c-9493-41180180f832" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323127} {:answer/id #uuid "22083d22-ec9e-5ce0-8088-576634d5dee2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323128}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ea13a5d2-1ece-567c-9493-41180180f832" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323127} :answer-field/id #uuid "2151a3fd-7d07-59af-883b-cb42191421d9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323123}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "03e34d98-2bed-5c40-b736-989a8742687c" :question/math-academy-id "q-74049" :question/problem "Suppose $(a+b)\\cdot a=0$, where $a\\ne 0.$ Which of the following statements are true? \n\n- $a\\cdot a<0$\n- $b\\cdot a=a\\cdot a$\n- $b\\cdot a<0$" :question/worked-solution "Let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Since $a\\ne 0$, we must have $a\\cdot a>0.$\n\n- Statement II is false while statement III is true. Simplifying the expression $(a+b)\\cdot a=0$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(a+b)\\cdot a & =0 \\\\ a\\cdot a+b\\cdot a & =0 \\\\ b\\cdot a & =-a\\cdot a\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nSince $a\\ne 0$ implies that $a\\cdot a>0$, we obtain that $-a\\cdot a<0.$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the correct answer is \"III only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323129 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "41c2d289-8df5-551b-9501-3a762c67386f" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About the Dot Product"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dc8f239f-69da-5ecd-959f-45e2eaeb1696" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323131} {:answer/id #uuid "745aa1d7-57e6-52b3-abdc-0368451d846f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323132} {:answer/id #uuid "77614521-9142-5910-a9b3-34eede9db7c3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323133} {:answer/id #uuid "4e2a91b3-d467-5f4a-b2bb-2812dbae0f4f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323134} {:answer/id #uuid "81652891-1c3d-50c4-a73a-6b6417913824" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323135}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4e2a91b3-d467-5f4a-b2bb-2812dbae0f4f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323134} :answer-field/id #uuid "65463832-816e-56f0-9817-a6d6a94d33bf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323130}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "926ef043-60dd-5c02-8791-e1cdde5c2ebe" :question/math-academy-id "q-74051" :question/problem "Suppose $(a+b)\\cdot a=0$, where $a\\ne 0.$ Which of the following statements are true? \n\n- $a\\cdot a=0$\n- $a\\cdot b>0$\n- $a\\cdot b<0$" :question/worked-solution "Let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Since $a\\ne 0$, we must have $a\\cdot a>0.$\n\n- Statement II is false while statement III is true. Simplifying the expression $(a+b)\\cdot a=0$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(a+b)\\cdot a & =0 \\\\ a\\cdot a+b\\cdot a & =0 \\\\ a\\cdot a+a\\cdot b & =0 \\\\ a\\cdot b & =-a\\cdot a\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nSince $a\\ne 0$ implies that $a\\cdot a>0$, we obtain that $-a\\cdot a<0.$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the correct answer is \"III only\"."}]}
